# Lyo Monorepo Core Rules

## Layout & architecture (taxonomy-first)

- Solution is `Lyo.Net/Lyo.slnx` (XML). Projects live under top-level areas: `Core`, `Data`, `Security`, `Communication`, `Integration`, `Plugins`, `Apps`, `Tools`. Solution folders mirror disk paths.
- Every package is classified by archetype. Before adding or moving a package, read `Lyo.Net/docs/package-layout.md` and follow its classification checklist:
  - A = canonical Lyo domain → `Core/{Domain}/` (e.g. `Lyo.People.Models`)
  - B = capability + vendor provider → `Communication|Security/{Capability}/` (e.g. `Lyo.Translation.Google`)
  - C = thin vendor client → `Integration/{Vendor}/`, named `Lyo.{Vendor}.{X}.Client`
  - D = vendor product vertical → `Integration/{Vendor}/` (e.g. `Lyo.Discord.*`)
  - E = platform → `Plugins/*`, `Apps/*`, `Data/*`, `Tools/*`
- Dependency law (hard rule): `Core` must NEVER reference `Apps` platform packages, `Integration` vendors, vendor SDKs, or vendor clients. `Communication`/`Security` may reference `Core` and `Integration` vendor clients. Hosts wire vendor client → mapper → Core store.
- Suffix conventions: `.Models` (contracts/DTOs), `.Postgres` (EF Core persistence), `.Client` (HTTP/SDK wrapper), `.Web.Components` (Blazor), `.Tests` (xUnit), `.Benchmarks` (BenchmarkDotNet).

## C# style

- File-scoped namespaces, primary constructors, collection expressions, pattern matching, target-typed `new`. Use C# 14 `extension(IServiceCollection services)` blocks for extension members.
- Null-guard public entry points with `ArgumentHelpers.ThrowIfNull(...)` from `Lyo.Exceptions` (netstandard2.0-safe) — not `ArgumentNullException.ThrowIfNull`.
- XML doc comments on all public API. Docs explain usage and prerequisites, not just what the member does.
- Exceptions are one-liners: `public sealed class FooException(string message) : Exception(message);`
- Async methods accept and propagate `CancellationToken`.
- Follow `.editorconfig`: prefer `var`, braces only when multiline, expression bodies for simple members, max line length 180.

## DI & configuration pattern

Copy the pattern in `Lyo.Net/Plugins/Reporting/Lyo.Reporting.Postgres/Extensions.cs`:

- Each package exposes an `Extensions.cs` with fluent `Add{Feature}` members returning `IServiceCollection`, in three shapes: `Action<TOptions>` overload, options-instance overload, and `Add{Feature}FromConfiguration(IConfiguration, string sectionName = TOptions.SectionName)`.
- FromConfiguration inlines bind (`var options = new TOptions(); configuration.GetSection(sectionName).Bind(options);`) then calls the instance `Add*` overload. Missing sections are not errors: `Bind` on an absent section is a no-op, so declared defaults remain.
- Options classes are strongly typed with a `SectionName` constant and a `Validate()` method. The instance `Add*` overload calls `options.Validate()` at registration (Action / FromConfiguration funnel into it). Register both `IOptions<T>` (`Options.Create(options)` / `AddOptions`) and `T` (`TryAddSingleton(options)` or a factory that unwraps `IOptions<T>.Value`). Consumers take `T`, not `IOptions<T>`. Use `IOptionsMonitor<T>` only when values reload (auth scheme handlers, Config API remote options). Do not use `AddOptions<T>().Bind().ValidateOnStart()` as the house path.
- Use `TryAddEnumerable(ServiceDescriptor.Singleton<IFace, Impl>())` for multi-implementation interfaces and `TryAdd*` for defaults the host may override.
